{"ts": 1789771246.257068, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "Sign flips confirm the orientations are right (0.53 vs 1.14). I'll submit `{0:+, 24:+, 27:\u2212}`.\n\nNow for calibrating the prediction. A few considerations argue for shrinking well below the 1.139 in-sample figure:\n\n- I ran ~60 evaluations and picked the max, so the winner captures upward selection noise. With n=5000 daily obs (~20 years), the standard error on an annualized Sharpe is roughly \u221a(1/20) \u2248 0.22 per spec. Maximizing over many correlated candidates adds maybe 0.1\u20130.2 of optimistic bias.\n- Reassuringly, the top ~8 triples all land in 0.97\u20131.14, a tight plateau rather than one spiky outlier. That suggests genuine signal, not a single lucky draw \u2014 the winner is likely only modestly overstated relative to its neighbors.\n- The components are individually strong (0.82, 0.64, \u22120.56) and combine with real diversification (1.14 > any single), so the effect isn't an artifact of one feature.\n- The status bar (1.022) is search-adjusted and I clear it, but only by ~0.12.\n\nBalancing the genuine plateau against selection bias and possible in-sample/out-of-sample regime drift, I'll center my belief near 0.85 with a fairly wide SD of 0.30 \u2014 covering outcomes from mediocre (~0.3) to roughly in-sample (~1.15)."}
