{"ts": 1789771287.592732, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "## Summary\n\n**Submitted specification:** long feature 0, long feature 24, short feature 27 \u2014 in-sample Sharpe **1.139** (bar: 1.022).\n\n**Method:**\n1. Screened all 40 features individually. Most were noise (|Sharpe| < 0.2); a handful stood out: F0 (+0.82), F24 (+0.64), F27 (\u22120.56), F14 (+0.52), F36 (\u22120.46).\n2. Tested pairs among the leaders \u2014 best was {0,24} at 1.016, a genuine gain over F0 alone, indicating imperfectly correlated signals.\n3. Extended to triples. The top candidates formed a tight plateau at 1.10\u20131.14 rather than one isolated spike.\n4. Verified sign orientation: flipping signs collapsed Sharpe from 1.14 to ~0.53, confirming the directions are real and not arbitrary.\n\n**Prediction: 0.85 \u00b1 0.30**, deliberately shrunk from the 1.139 in-sample value.\n\nThe main reason for the haircut is that I selected the maximum over ~60 evaluations, so the winner embeds upward selection noise. With n=5000, the standard error on an annualized Sharpe is ~0.22 for a *single* pre-specified spec; picking the best of many correlated candidates adds further optimistic bias. Reporting 1.139 as an OOS expectation would be exactly the overfitting error this setup invites.\n\nWhat kept me from shrinking further is that the evidence looks structurally sound: the components are individually strong, they combine with real diversification benefit, and the top triples cluster tightly instead of one lucky outlier towering over the rest. That pattern is more consistent with actual signal than with noise mining.\n\nOne caveat worth flagging: the gap over the search-adjusted bar is only ~0.12, which is modest relative to my uncertainty. My SD of 0.30 is wide on purpose \u2014 it spans from mediocre (~0.3) to roughly in-sample (~1.15), and I would not be shocked by an OOS result at either end, particularly if there is regime drift between the in-sample and holdout periods that I have no way to observe from here."}
